<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bakhchysarai Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khan's Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a> Palace, or Khan's Palace, is the residence of the Crimean khans at Bakhchysarai, begun in the 16th century. Its courtyards, mosques, harem, cemetery and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchisaray-fountain">Fountain of Tears</a>, which inspired a poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a>, reflect Ottoman and Persian influence. Largely rebuilt after Russian troops burned it in 1736, it is the main surviving monument of the Crimean Khanate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai-palace.jpg" alt="Bakhchysarai Palace" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chapultepec · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hansaray1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Khan's Palace, in Crimean Tatar Hansaray, is in the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. The Crimean Khan dynasty commissioned both the town and the palace and moved their capital there from Salaçıq in the first half of the 16th century. The palace's complex design and its minarets were constructed in that century by Ottoman, Persian and Italian architects. It became the home of a succession of Crimean khans. Some buildings now in the palace were added later, while some of the original ones did not survive past the 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The walled enclosure contains a mosque, a harem, a cemetery, living quarters and gardens. Its interior has been decorated to look lived in and reflects the traditional 16th-century Crimean Tatar style. The Big Khan Mosque, built in 1532 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahib-i-giray">Sahib I Giray</a>, is one of the largest mosques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. It has a three-aisle square prayer hall and two octagonal minarets twenty-eight metres high. A fire damaged it in 1736 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1735-1739">Russo-Turkish War</a>, and it was restored under Khan Selâmet II Giray.</p><p>The Small Khan Mosque, in the main building, was designed for the Khan's family and important dignitaries. Its mihrab has an upper part cut with seven ornamented belts, symbolising the seven levels of heaven, and above it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stained-glass">stained glass</a> window shows the seal of Suleiman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>A small fountain in one courtyard moved the Russian writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Alexander Pushkin</a> so much that he wrote the long narrative poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fountain-of-bakhchisaray">The Fountain of Bakhchisaray</a>. By the story told of it, one of the last Crimean khans, Qırım Giray, fell in love with a Polish girl named Maria in his harem, and after her early death he is said to have wept and commissioned a marble fountain so that the rock would weep like him forever. It was moved to the Ambassadors' courtyard after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> ordered the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. Pushkin's verses are credited in part with ensuring the palace's survival. It was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Tentative List of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bakhchysarai Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In December 1917 an elected national assembly, the Kurultai, met in the <b>Khan's Palace</b> at Bakhchysarai, adopted a constitution and proclaimed the Crimean People's Republic, with the mufti Noman Celebicihan as its leader.</blockquote><a href="/state/crimean-peoples-republic">Crimean People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
